[Music]  when we're dealing with repeated notes  there are a few things to consider  depending on the context of the music  the first thing is whether or not we're  using different fingers or repeating the  same finger so for example the scarlatti  Sonata which is very famous for having  the repeated notes we're using four  three two one or three two one three two  one whatever combination suits you but  the key is that we're using different  fingers to play the same note side note  I like to use four three two one two one  because of the rhythmic implications I  don't like how it's divided into two  when I'm using three two one three two  one but I think both can work the thing  to note about the mechanics of repeated  notes is pretty simple you basically  need to get your fingers out of the way  so that the note the key can actually  reset before the next note is played  otherwise it'll get stuck which is a  common problem with repeated notes  [Music]  so this is a very obvious thing but when  you're using multiple fingers you have  an advantage because while you're  playing one note and you're getting it  out of the way the next note can be  ready to play right there  whereas when you're playing with one  finger and trying to repeat it regard  this is of how you're playing it there's  that time required to get it back to  position now this brings me to another  point so there's a big difference  between playing then though vertically  so with an up-and-down motion and  playing with curved fingers the thing to  note is that when you're playing up and  down even though it seems like you're  not moving as much you're actually  moving directions you're going up and  down whereas when you're playing with  the curved finger the change of  direction is softened because you're  using this circular motion so say that  you're repeating a note with three two  one  instead of thinking of it as a pecking  motion like this in that case maybe  you'll be using a wrist or your hands  more and you're inviting your hands to  tense up instead of that think of it as  a coil or some sort of spiral regardless  of what finger you're using so 4 3 2 3 -  even just one finger so this is a  measure from a piece that I wrote where  the top end requires a repeated note  with the fifth finger  [Music]  and in this case I think the type of  technique that you're using is very  similar to repeated octaves which I'll  probably do a video on separately  technique really comes from a very  relaxed wrist so even though visually it  may seem like my wrists aren't that  loose they are pretty loose compared to  my elbows which my whole forearm is  anchored from and they're locked into  place so my elbows are locked and my  wrists are relatively loose so now let's  go to the exercise  [Music]  so I wanted to create an exercise where  I'm challenging myself by using  different pairs of fingers for repeated  notes and in this case I settled with  tu-154 after that five four three two  and then I added in some intervals and  I'm just cycling through the circle of  this the most challenging thing for me  is when I'm repeating Black Keys just  because the surface area is much smaller  I have to be much more focused so of  course we don't want to neglect the left  hand so there's a left hand version as  well  [Music]  so just like with any other type of  technique or figure when we're  practicing repeated notes we can use  different methods such as rhythmic  practicing different groupings but the  thing to keep in mind is what you're  actually doing at the fingertips in  order to repeat the note and not get  caught up so whether we're playing  groups of two or with thoughted rhythms  or just slowly I'm going to focus my  attention on what I mentioned earlier  about the curved fingers and that  spinning type of motion almost like a  motor and also being very consistent  about the point of contact for each note  so I don't want to be repeating the note  in different areas of the keys just for  the sake of efficiency so I'll keep  these things in mind while practicing an  exercise like this or any other music  that requires repeated notes  [Music]  here's another repeated not exercise  that I've shown in one of my previous  videos that was taught to me by one of  my professors you just take one note and  you repeat it using all five fingers and  you'll be accenting every force note so  that you'll be cycling through accenting  with each finger you'll be basically  doing this while increasing the speed  gradually so anyway I hope you try these  exercises and let me know what you think  and as always I'll see you in the next  video bye  you

